{
  "note": "The codebook of experiment 54, and the labels a reader gave by hand to a spread of 59 failed reviews (108 failing verdicts). `reasons` labels a failing verdict as a whole, by its one-line reason. `findings` labels the points the splitter cut it into, by `<comment id>#<n>`. A label is `kind`, or `kind/restsOn`. Links are built as https://github.com/kamp-us/phoenix/pull/<n>#issuecomment-<comment id>.",
  "kind": {
    "instructions": "`finding` is one point from a code reviewer's failed verdict on a pull request. `reason` is the reviewer's one-line reason for the whole verdict. Choose what kind of problem `finding` is. If `finding` is only background, or does not say what is wrong, go by `reason`.",
    "choices": {
      "stale_text": "Text the change left behind. A doc, skill page, help line, message or comment that the change did not update still says the old thing, and is now false.",
      "wrong_text": "New text that is wrong. A sentence, table or example the change itself wrote says something the code or the facts do not support, or contradicts itself.",
      "code_bug": "The code does the wrong thing in some case: a logic error, a wrong default, a branch that cannot run, lost data, a message that sends the user the wrong way.",
      "check_red": "A check a machine ran is red at this commit: CI, a named test, a repo guard, or a size or budget limit fails. Or the branch conflicts, so the checks could not run.",
      "decision_conflict": "The change does something a standing decision record (ADR) or the glossary forbids, or decided differently.",
      "record_keeping": "A decision record's own paperwork is wrong: a duplicate or mismatched id, a status line not updated, an older record not marked as amended or superseded, a missing citation.",
      "criterion_unmet": "The ticket asked for something and the change plainly did not do it, or did the opposite. An acceptance criterion, or a ruling a person posted on the ticket.",
      "convention": "A written repo or pipeline convention is broken: a naming rule, a rule about how tests or docs are written, a required tag or line.",
      "test_gap": "A test is missing for new behaviour, or a test checks the wrong thing.",
      "pr_text": "The pull request's own text or the builder's report is wrong or incomplete: a disclosure in the wrong place, a count that is off, a claim that no longer holds.",
      "prose_craft": "Only the writing. A sentence that is hard to read, bad wrapping, jargon. Nothing is false.",
      "carried": "No finding of its own. This part of the review says its own files are clean, and fails only because another part found something or a criterion was added this round.",
      "not_a_finding": "Not a blocking problem. It says something is fine, fixed or checked, gives background or routing, or says outright that it does not block."
    }
  },
  "restsOn": {
    "instructions": "`finding` is one point from a code reviewer's failed verdict on a pull request. `reason` is the reviewer's one-line reason for the whole verdict. Choose where the fact that makes it a problem comes from.",
    "choices": {
      "check": "A machine already said so: CI or a named test is red, a guard, lint or budget check reports it, or a command reports a duplicate or a conflict.",
      "written_rule": "A rule written down in the repository: a decision record (ADR), a pattern, convention or rubric page, a glossary.",
      "ticket": "The ticket itself: one of its acceptance criteria, or a ruling a person posted on it.",
      "reading": "None of those. The reviewer saw it by reading the change and comparing code, docs and facts, or by trying it out."
    }
  },
  "needed": {
    "instructions": "`finding` is one point from a code reviewer's failed verdict on a pull request. `reason` is the reviewer's one-line reason for the whole verdict. Choose what it took to find this problem.",
    "choices": {
      "code": "A machine check shows it, with no reading: CI or a named test is red, a guard, lint or size limit fails, a command reports a duplicate id or a merge conflict.",
      "brief": "It breaks a rule that is written down, and seeing it only took knowing the rule: a decision record (ADR), a convention or pattern page, the glossary, an acceptance criterion of the ticket, or a ruling on the ticket.",
      "reader": "It took reading and judgment: comparing code with docs or comments, tracing what the code does, trying it out, or judging the writing.",
      "none": "No problem of its own: its files are clean and it fails only because of another part or an added criterion, or it is a note that something is fine or fixed."
    }
  },
